<header><p class="st-kicker">princely state · Indian princely states</p><h1 class="st-title">Loharu State</h1><p class="st-meta">1806 CE – 1947 CE · capital Loharu</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A small Muslim-ruled state on the edge of the Thar, granted in 1806 to the family into which the poet Ghalib married.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahmad-bakhsh-khan">Ahmad Bakhsh Khan</a>, an agent who helped negotiate between the British and the ruler of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/alwar-state">Alwar State</a> during the war of 1803, received Loharu from Alwar and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/firozpur-jhirka">Firozpur Jhirka</a> from the British in 1806 as a reward.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>His son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shamsuddin-ahmad-khan">Shamsuddin Ahmad Khan</a> was hanged in 1835 for arranging the murder of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-fraser-delhi">William Fraser</a>, the British Resident at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi">Delhi</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/firozpur-jhirka">Firozpur Jhirka</a> was confiscated, leaving the family only Loharu. The Nawabs ruled a small dry territory with a mostly <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> peasantry. The poet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mirza-ghalib">Mirza Ghalib</a> married into the family and was closely tied to its Delhi household.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Loharu acceded to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> in 1947 and was merged into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-punjab">East Punjab</a>; it is now in Bhiwani district of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haryana">Haryana</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/loharu-state.jpg" alt="Loharu State" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: Germenfer · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_the_Princely_State_of_Loharu.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1806 CE – 1947 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>princely state</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Loharu</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Ahmad Bakhsh Khan, Shamsuddin Ahmad Khan</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Hinduism; Sunni Islam (ruling house)</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Haryanvi, Urdu</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/india">India</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Loharu</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1876&amp;focus=loharu-state" aria-label="Loharu State on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6664 1136 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Loharu State lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M6984 1392L7364 1601M6984 1392L7364 1602M6984 1392L7018 1386"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M7364 1601h0M7364 1602h0M7018 1386h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6984 1392h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6984 1392h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Loharu. <a href="/atlas?year=1876&amp;focus=loharu-state">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
